Manual check that the new `run_test` wrapper (tests/fm-captain-hold-lifecycle.test.sh)
actually detects a leaked `set -e` rather than passing vacuously, per the
change's claim: "it fires and names the guilty test."

Reintroduced the exact pattern the fix removed into a scratch copy of the
suite's first test (test_uninventoried_report_decision_refuses_completion):

    -  run_teardown "$home" "$id" > "$home/teardown.out" 2> "$home/teardown.err"
    -  rc=$?
    +  set +e
    +  run_teardown "$home" "$id" > "$home/teardown.out" 2> "$home/teardown.err"
    +  rc=$?
    +  set -e

Ran the patched copy (temporarily placed in tests/ so it could source
tests/lib.sh, then deleted immediately after):

$ bash tests/fm-captain-hold-lifecycle-LEAK-CHECK.test.sh
ok - report-only unresolved captain call is reproduced and completion refuses before loss
not ok - errexit leaked out of test_uninventoried_report_decision_refuses_completion: later tests would abort silently instead of reporting
$ echo "exit: $?"
exit: 1

Result: the guard fires on the very next check after the leak is
introduced, names the exact test function that leaked errexit, prints a
`not ok` line, and the run exits 1 instead of continuing silently. This
confirms the guard is not vacuous.
